 source', 'all_citations': ['38 U.S.C. § 4316'], 'primary_citation': '38 U.S.C. § 4316', 'case_name': 'N/A', 'format_analysis': {'format_type': 'valid', 'is_valid_format': True, 'is_valid_volume': True, 'error': None}, 'likelihood_score': 0.5, 'explanation': 'Citation format is valid but not found in searched databases', 'context': 'rden’s absence for military service—or any authority indicating how that should affect his entitlement to paid military leave under RCW 38.40.060. But see Dkt. 35, at 129-34 (declaration of Dani Smith) (attaching military orders requiring Bearden to report for training or duty). Moreover, under USERRA, a person who is absent from a position of employment by reason of service in the uniformed services is “deemed to be on furlough or leave of absence while performing such service.” 38 U.S.C. § 4316(b)(1)(A). Entitlement to benefits while on such leave of absence “is not dependent on how the employer characterizes the employee’s status during a period of service.” 20 C.F.R. § 1002.149. Though Bearden was also employed by the Army, the record indicates he continued to be an employee of the city while on 12 Bearden v. City of Ocean Shores et al. No. 103121-1 leave of absence for military service. The city’s argument about the duration of Bearden’s military service is similarly unavailing. Th'}, {'verified': 'false', 'canonical_citation': '20 C.F.R. § 1002.149', 'url': None, 'extracted_case_name': 'N/A', 'hinted_case_name': None, 'extracted_date': '', 'canonical_date': None, 'error': 'Invalid citation format: Unrecognized citation format', 'all_citations': ['20 C.F.R. § 1002.149'], 'primary_citation': '20 C.F.R. § 1002.149', 'case_name': 'N/A', 'format_analysis': {'format_type': 'unknown', 'is_valid_format': False, 'is_valid_volume': False, 'error': 'Unrecognized citation format'}, 'likelihood_score': 0.1, 'explanation': 'Invalid citation format: Unrecognized citation format', 'context': '-34 (declaration of Dani Smith) (attaching military orders requiring Bearden to report for training or duty). Moreover, under USERRA, a person who is absent from a position of employment by reason of service in the uniformed services is “deemed to be on furlough or leave of absence while performing such service.” 38 U.S.C. § 4316(b)(1)(A). Entitlement to benefits while on such leave of absence “is not dependent on how the employer characterizes the employee’s status during a period of service.” 20 C.F.R. § 1002.149. Though Bearden was also employed by the Army, the record indicates he continued to be an employee of the city while on 12 Bearden v. City of Ocean Shores et al. No. 103121-1 leave of absence for military service. The city’s argument about the duration of Bearden’s military service is similarly unavailing. The plain language of RCW 38.40.060 places no temporal limit on eligibility for paid military leave but, rather, provides that public employees are eligible for paid military leave “each year'}, {'verified': 'false', 'canonical_citation': '38 U.S.C. § 4312', 'url': None, 'extracted_case_name': 'N/A', 'hinted_case_name': None, 'extracted_date': '', 'canonical_date': None, 'error': 'Citation could not be verified by any source', 'all_citations': ['38 U.S.C. § 4312'], 'primary_citation': '38 U.S.C. § 4312', 'case_name': 'N/A', 'format_analysis': {'format_type': 'valid', 'is_valid_format': True, 'is_valid_volume': True, 'error': None}, 'likelihood_score': 0.5, 'explanation': 'Citation format is valid but not found in searched databases', 'context': 'e extent the city worries a person might go on active military duty for year after year, even decades, only to return to their public employer and claim all the benefits they might have accrued in the intervening years, that hypothetical is not presented by the facts or certified question in this case. Even so, USERRA generally limits reemployment rights to a maximum of five years’ cumulative absence, so it appears there are legal guardrails in place to account for such a hypothetical scenario. 38 U.S.C. § 4312(a)(2); see also RCW 73.16.035(1)(e) (four years). 13 Bearden v. City of Ocean Shores et al. No. 103121-1 that the legislature means exactly what it says.” DeSean v. Sanger, 2 Wn.3d 329, 335, 536 P.3d 191 (2023) (citing State v. Delgado, 148 Wn.2d 723, 727, 63 P.3d 792 (2003)); see also, e.g., Duke v. Boyd, 133 Wn.2d 80, 87, 942 P.2d 351'}, {'verified': 'true', 'case_name': 'State v. Roggenkamp', 'canonical_name': 'State v. Roggenkamp', 'citation': '153 Wash. 2d 614 106 P.3d 196 148 Wash. 2d 338 60 P.3d 586 141 Wash. 2d 139 3 P.3d 741', 'canonical_citation': '153 Wash. 2d 614 106 P.3d 196 148 Wash. 2d 338 60 P.3d 586 141 Wash. 2d 139 3 P.3d 741', 'url': 'https://www.courtlistener.com/opinion/4908403/state-v-roggenkamp/', 'canonical_date': '2005-02-10', 'court': '', 'docket_number': '', 'source': 'CourtListener', 'confidence': 0.9, 'parallel_citations': [{'citation': '153 Wash. 2d 614', 'reporter': 'Wash. 2d', 'category': 2, 'volume': '153', 'page': '614', 'url': ''}], 'verification_method': 'courtlistener_api', 'extracted_case_name': 'State v. Roggenkamp', 'hinted_case_name': None, 'extracted_date': '', 'context': ''}, {'verified': 'true', 'case_name': 'Densley v. Department of Retirement Systems', 'canonical_name': 'Densley v. Department of Retirement Systems', 'citation': '162 Wash. 2d 210 173 P.3d 885', 'canonical_citation': '162 Wash. 2d 210 173 P.3d 885', 'url': 'https://www.courtlistener.com/opinion/4908756/densley-v-department-of-retirement-systems/', 'canonical_date': '2007-11-15', 'court': '', 'docket_number': '', 'source': 'CourtListener', 'confidence': 0.9, 'parallel_citations': [{'citation': '162 Wash. 2d 210', 'reporter': 'Wash. 2d', 'category': 2, 'volume': '162', 'page': '210', 'url': ''}], 'verification_method': 'courtlistener_api', 'extracted_case_name': 'Densley v. Department of Retirement Systems', 'hinted_case_name': None, 'extracted_date': '', 'context': ''}, {'verified': 'true', 'case_name': 'Associ